    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Health-Campus/Campaign-and-Volunteer-Engagement-Manager--Adjunct----Institutional-Advancement_R105075": {
      "title": "Campaign and Volunteer Engagement Manager (Adjunct) - Institutional Advancement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPosition Summary:   This position leads the day-to-day coordination of campaign related activities and campaign volunteer engagement, serving as a key partner to campaign leadership, volunteers, fundraisers, and University partners. This role advances campaign fundraising efforts by coordinating and tracking activity, progress, and follow-through toward campaign goals. This position oversees volunteer communications and meetings, develops materials and resources to equip volunteers for success, coordinates campaign visits and solicitations, and facilitates collaboration across teams. This position anticipates needs, identifies opportunities to strengthen engagement, and ensures campaign priorities are effectively organized and advanced in support of the Comprehensive Campaign. Salary & Benefits:   Minimum starting salary of $60,000, commensurate with experience . Limited benefits package including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  Primary Duties:   Serve as a primary point of contact for campaign volunteer engagement and support, providing guidance, resources, and information; monitoring engagement; and escalating complex matters to campaign leadership. In collaboration with colleagues in prospect research and management and frontline fundraisers, coordinate and track campaign prospects, assignments, cultivation activities, solicitations, and follow-up actions. Manage the preparation and execution of campaign volunteer meetings, positioning campaign leadership for successful engagement with campaign volunteers. Partner with professional support staff on scheduling, materials, communications, and follow-up to facilitate effective implementation. Facilitate cross-functional communication and workflow among campaign leadership, Institutional Advancement teams, volunteers, and University partners. Ensure prospect research and management needs, requests, assignments, timelines, and relevant information are effectively communicated, routed, and advanced through completion. Drive the prospect visit and solicitation process forward by coordinating with frontline fundraisers and professional support staff to ensure timely completion of briefing materials, proposals, solicitation tracking, and follow-up communications. Develop and maintain organized campaign resources and systems, including virtual and hardcopy volunteer resources, prospect materials, assignment information, campaign communications, meeting outcomes, and other documentation essential to effective campaign execution. Track and communicate campaign progress and activity through regular review of volunteer engagement, prospect assignments, solicitation activity, outstanding action items, and key deadlines. Identify gaps and opportunities for follow-up and prepare reports, updates, and materials for campaign leadership. Advance campaign-related processes and special projects, including collaboration with colleagues in gift processing to support accurate recordkeeping and timely processing of campaign gifts. Contribute to volunteer engagement initiatives, campaign events, communications, data collection, and other priorities supporting successful implementation of the Comprehensive Campaign. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Bachelor’s degree or equivalent combination of education and relevant experience in an appropriate field such as communications, marketing, business, nonprofit management, or a related discipline. Two to five (2-5) years of volunteer management and project management experience, with the ability to develop and maintain effective systems, manage multiple priorities and deadlines, track assignments and commitments, and ensure timely follow-through. Excellent interpersonal skills and communication skills, with demonstrated ability to build effective, collaborative relationships that advance work and achieve shared goals with volunteers, senior leaders, staff, internal and external partners. Proven ability to work proactively and collaboratively across multiple teams, anticipate needs, identify gaps or potential issues, facilitate the flow of information, and exercise sound judgment in determining appropriate follow-up. Ability to work independently and effectively in a fast-paced, evolving environment while managing competing demands, exercising professionalism, and maintaining confidentiality. Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Knowledge of advancement, fundraising, comprehensive campaign, volunteer engagement, or donor relations practices and processes. Experience using advancement databases, project tracking systems, communication platforms, or other technology used to coordinate projects, manage information, and track activity. Working Conditions:   Normal office environment. Some travel and evening/weekend work may be required.    How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Institutional Advancement at advancement@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline:   Application review begins October 1, 2026. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Health-Campus/Regional-Director-of-Philanthropy-Southeast-Michigan--Adjunct----Institutional-Advancement_R105069": {
      "title": "Regional Director of Philanthropy Southeast Michigan (Adjunct) - Institutional Advancement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPosition Summary:   This position is responsible for cultivating and expanding relationships with alumni, parents, friends, and supporters of Grand Valley State University (GVSU) throughout Southeast Michigan. This position plays an important role in building GVSU's presence and philanthropic pipeline in the region by identifying new prospects, strengthening relationships with existing supporters, and creating meaningful opportunities for individuals to engage with and invest in the university. Working as part of the Philanthropy Team and in partnership with academic and university leaders, this position develops and implements strategies to identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward individual donors. This role has significant responsibility for managing a portfolio of prospective and current donors and for developing new relationships. Salary & Benefits:   Minimum starting salary of $95,000 commensurate with experience . Limited benefits package including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  Primary Duties:   Develop and implement an annual plan for the Southeast Michigan region to identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward prospective and current donors in support of short- and long-term university fundraising goals. Serve as a primary relationship manager for a portfolio of prospects and donors, including individuals with the capacity to make gifts of $15,000 or more annually. Develop individualized strategies to deepen relationships, identify philanthropic interests, and advance prospects through the fundraising cycle. Build and expand GVSU's alumni and philanthropic network throughout Southeast Michigan by identifying new prospects, engaging alumni and supporters, and developing relationships with community leaders. Partner with university and academic leaders, faculty, volunteers, and colleagues across Institutional Advancement, including the areas of corporate giving and foundation relations, to develop compelling opportunities for philanthropic investment that align donor interests with university priorities. Manage and advance major gift opportunities, including developing strategies, coordinating internal resources, preparing proposals and solicitation materials, and supporting successful gift requests. Lead or coordinate projects related to complex philanthropic gifts, working collaboratively with colleagues in Philanthropy, Donor Relations, Gift Planning, Corporate Giving and Foundation Relations. Prepare and execute major gift materials, including briefing materials, proposals, stewardship communications, and other donor-facing materials. Collaborate with Donor Relations to develop appropriate stewardship strategies for donors and prospects. Track and report relationship management activity using the advancement CRM, including identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship activity. Provide regular updates regarding portfolio activity, opportunities, and progress toward fundraising goals. Maintain accurate and timely records of donor and prospect interactions, including contact reports, key conversations, strategies, and next steps. Maintain an active portfolio of prospects and donors with capacity for significant philanthropic support and consistently move relationships forward through meaningful engagement. Represent Grand Valley State University and the Grand Valley University Foundation at university events, alumni activities, community gatherings, volunteer opportunities, and professional organizations throughout Southeast Michigan. Perform other duties as required or assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Bachelor's degree or knowledge equivalent to that which normally would be acquired by completing a four-year college degree program in an appropriate field (e.g., communications, public relations, marketing, business, nonprofit management, or related field). Five to seven (5-7) years of fundraising, advancement, relationship management, sales, business development, community engagement, or related experience. Demonstrated experience to independently identify, qualify, and build a portfolio of new donor relationships rather than relying on an established donor base. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain meaningful relationships with individuals from diverse backgrounds and levels of professional experience. Ability to identify areas of shared interest between an individual's passions and the priorities and mission of the university. Understanding of, or demonstrated ability to learn, the principles of successful fundraising, including prospect identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship. Strong interpersonal and human relations skills, with the ability to effectively interact with university leadership, faculty, alumni, donors, volunteers, and community members. Ability to work independently, take initiative, manage competing priorities, and follow through on commitments. Curiosity and a desire to learn about donors, the university, and the communities in which GVSU operates. Ability to communicate in a clear, compelling, and persuasive manner that builds confidence and trust. Ability to develop and manage projects from inception through completion. Computer proficiency, including working knowledge of CRM databases and standard office technology. Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Master’s degree. Successful higher education fundraising experience in raising major gifts. Prior experience working with senior-level executives and professionals. Strategic mindset: ability to be forward-thinking and to create long-term relationships for maximum gifts, rather than focusing on single transactions. Ability to adapt within formal, confidential, and various business and social settings. Proven successful experience working in a complex environment on multiple and diverse projects. Working Conditions:   Travel throughout Southeast Michigan and work evenings and weekends as needed. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Institutional Advancement at advancement@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline:   Application closes Monday October 19, 2026. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Student-Care-and-Outreach-Case-Manager---Dean-of-Students-Office_R105057": {
      "title": "Student Care and Outreach Case Manager - Dean of Students Office",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nThis position provides nonclinical case management support to students referred to the Dean of Students Office. This role works directly with students to help identify concerns and connect them with appropriate campus and community resources. Working under the guidance of senior staff, this role supports students experiencing a range of personal challenges, including family emergencies, mental health or medical concerns, financial or basic needs, and academic difficulties. The position collaborates with campus partners to promote student well-being and persistence. This is a 0.8 FTE position. The Student Care and Outreach Case Manager will work a standard 40-hour/week schedule during the Fall and Winter semesters and reduce to a 6.25 hours/week schedule during the months of June and July. Salary & Benefits:   $40,240 - $ 44,240 ,   commensurate   with experience   Comprehensive benefits package   including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness   resources   and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.   Primary Duties: Under the direction of leadership, provide direct support to students through case management services, including resource referral, follow-up, and basic self-advocacy support. Provide outreach, educational opportunities, and other student services as appropriate. Collaborate with campus partners within Student Affairs and across the university and participate as a member of the University CARE Team. Assist in providing consultation to faculty, staff, and supporters regarding student challenges, concerns, and appropriate referral options. Promote holistic development and integrated care to foster student success. Assist in maintaining relationships with community-based resources within the Allendale & Grand Rapids Community. Maintain all required documentation of activities within Maxient database system.    Support compliance with reporting requirements under Title IX, the Clery Act, and other applicable regulations, in consultation with supervisor. Demonstrate commitment to working with traditionally underserved or marginalized populations and assist in connecting students to appropriate campus support. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Bachelor’s degree in education, psychology, social work, or related field. One (1) year of relevant experience in nonclinical case management, student support, human services, or a related area (internships and graduate assistantships may be considered). Ability to work effectively with all constituents (students, faculty, administration, community members, parents and supporters). Demonstrated ability to exercise professional judgement and leadership, including the ability to work independently. Strong interpersonal skills, including strong oral, written, and listening communication capabilities. Ability to manage sensitive information and maintain confidentiality. Ability to establish and maintain professional working relationships with colleagues and be thoughtful of multicultural environment. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Master’s degree (completed or in progress) in student affairs, education, psychology, social work, or related field. Experience working in higher education environment or student support setting. Experience working with Maxient Database System. Experience working in the areas of crisis management, behavior intervention, and community standards in higher education environment. Working knowledge of Title IX, FERPA, and other regulations associated with the position. Demonstrated success in collaborating with campus wide stake holders. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Ben Witt, wittbe@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at  hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.    Application Deadline:   Application review begins October 6. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the university. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Data-Analyst---Office-of-the-Registrar-and-Institutional-Analysis--Adjunct-_R105064": {
      "title": "Data Analyst - Office of the Registrar and Institutional Analysis (Adjunct)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe GVSU Office of Institutional Analysis collects, analyzes, and disseminates information about the university’s students, processes, and resources. This position will design, develop, maintain, and document online data access tools and respond to requests for data and analysis. The position holder will collaborate extensively with data owners, end users, and the Office of Data and Analytics. This is a 12-month adjunct position. This position is expected to work in-office in Allendale, Michigan. Salary & Benefits:  Minimum starting salary $60,000, commensurate with experience Limited benefits package including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties: Migrate and modernize legacy online reporting tools to a new data lake/warehouse. Work with university Data Advisory body to design and develop new reporting tools in response to university needs and priorities. Assist in the development and maintenance of university’s analytic data repository. Collaborate in planning the office’s adaptation to new demands, new data sources, and new methodologies. Respond to routine and ad hoc requests for statistical and analytic information. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Bachelor’s degree with significant coursework in Research Methods and Statistical Analysis. Strong analytical and quantitative skills. Demonstrated ability to develop effective and responsive visualization tools for complex data. Experience with Excel, SQL, data visualization tools, and programming languages (e.g. Python, SAS). Ability to perform accurate and effective work with limited supervision. Ability to prioritize, plan, and organize multiple work assignments. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Experience in design and conduct of survey research. Familiarity with major university data systems (Banner, Slate, Workday). Knowledge of key web design building blocks (HTML, Javascript, CSS, JQuery). Working Conditions: Normal office environment.  Some travel may be required. How to Apply:  Attach your cover letter and resume. Applications missing required documents will not be considered .   If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Daniel Vainner ( vainneda@gvsu.edu ). If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline:  September 30, 2026 at 11:59 pm. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Early-Childhood-Education---College-of-Education-and-Community-Innovation_R105042": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Early Childhood Education - College of Education and Community Innovation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Early Childhood Education program at Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is looking for a tenure track faculty member to join the Department of Teaching and Learning in the College of Education & Community Innovation at the assistant professor level to serve primarily undergraduate students taking courses in early childhood education that also fulfill general education requirements. Primary responsibilities include: teaching undergraduate courses in early childhood and early childhood special education, both in person and using online modalities; supervising candidates in the field; participating in service obligations; and conducting meaningful research that will be disseminated to a diverse audience. For this particular position, expertise is especially needed as it relates to the philosophies, theories and historical foundations of early childhood education; the impacts on child development & how childhood is influenced by the current context; knowledge in the seminal texts of the field and contemporary insights; and movements that help lead to increased equity, culturally responsive and sustained teaching, and inclusive learning environments. Opportunities may also exist for teaching courses for graduate students and courses connected to observation and documentation, learning through play, child guidance, and collaborating with families. Salary Range & Benefits:   $64,000 - $66,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties:   Teach undergraduate courses in early childhood, both in person and using online synchronous and asynchronous modalities Supervise candidates in diverse settings and using online tools Maintain clear and professional communication with students and colleagues Use evidence-based teaching practices that emphasize active learning, inquiry, anti-bias education, and critical reflection, to support students as they learn to work effectively with young children and their families and future colleagues Engage in service activities at the program, unit, college, university, and community level Conduct and disseminate meaningful scholarship Required Qualifications and Education:   An earned PhD or EdD degree or ABD (with expected graduation by August 2027) by an accredited institution in Early Childhood Education, Early Childhood Special Education, Child Development, Instruction and Curriculum, or Teacher Education with an emphasis in Early Childhood, Family Studies, or Human or Child Development At least three years experience working with a range of young children and/or young children with disabilities and their families in an education setting (ages infant/toddler, preschool, and/or early elementary) The ability to consistently teach face to face courses on GVSU City and Valley campuses An interest in teaching coursework that addresses the philosophies, theories, and historical foundations of early childhood education The capacity to travel to provide clinical supervision in diverse settings The ability to engage with a variety of technologies to deliver instruction face to face and in online synchronous, asynchronous, and hybrid modalities Demonstrated excellence in delivering high quality and engaging instruction to adult learners A passion for teaching culturally relevant, responsive, and sustaining pedagogies and preparing educators to work with diverse populations A commitment to educating the whole child and promoting inclusive teaching practices Strong interpersonal communication and collaboration skills A research agenda, with the ability to disseminate findings to a scholarly audience An interest in working as part of a team in developing, implementing, and evaluating programs and activities that support programs in early childhood education An interest in contributing professional expertise to the community and profession Preferred Qualifications and Education:   Experience teaching adult learners at the university or college level, particularly as it relates to the Foundations and Philosophies of Early Childhood, Observation and Documentation, Child Guidance, Collaborating with Families, and Play-Based Curriculum courses Knowledge and familiarity with the seminal texts of the field, including those that relate to the history of education and more contemporary insights around the application of these principles More than five years experience working with diverse populations as a licensed educator within an early childhood setting, particularly in early elementary settings The ZA, ZS, or ZN teaching endorsement, as awarded by the Michigan Department of Education Experience or familiarity with teaching courses that meet General Education requirements and serve primarily first and second year students from a variety of academic disciplines Professional membership or involvement with NAEYC or CEC Experience teaching with the Blackboard Ultra Learning Management System A promising track record of disseminating research in peer-reviewed journals, books, and through scholarly presentations Experience with program evaluation and/or with accreditation standards Working Conditions:   Typical office environment, some travel may be required. How To Apply: Apply online at jobs.gvsu.edu and select \"Apply now\". Please include a (1) cover letter and (2) CV. The cover letter must address one’s areas of expertise, especially as it relates to one’s knowledge and use of theoretical frameworks to explain and enact early childhood pedagogies; one’s experience working with young children and their families; and how one has worked with adult learners thus far and what has or has not been effective in those teaching pursuits. The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically. On the application, you will be required to provide names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses for three professional references (to be used only in the final stage of consideration). Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact search committee chair Dr. Kathryn Ohle at ohlek@gvsu.edu .  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Application Deadline: Consideration of applications will begin immediately and the posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University; preference will be given to applications received by October 25th, 2026. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Earth-Surface-Materials-and-Systems---Department-of-Geology_R105055-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Earth Surface Materials and Systems - Department of Geology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Geology at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Earth Surface Materials and Systems to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in Geoscience or a closely related field. We are an exclusively undergraduate department that values a strong commitment to quality and inclusive teaching. We welcome adaptable candidates who have the capacity to lead a geology curriculum broadly in Earth Surface Materials, Geomorphology, and Hydrogeology. Candidates should also demonstrate a commitment to an active research program within those disciplines with the promise of successfully obtaining extramural support. The ideal candidate will have research interests that can directly integrate undergraduate students, with preference given to those with the potential to develop research within the Great Lakes region (e.g., surface water dynamics, glacial geomorphology, coastal processes, watershed hydrology, or groundwater). We also seek candidates focused on innovative modeling, field-based, or applied approaches and an interest in building notable external partnerships and/or interdisciplinary collaborations. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Salary/Benefits:  Minimum starting salary: $76,500, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package , including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver, effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. in the Geosciences or a closely related field. Potential for successful college-level teaching and active research in an undergraduate department. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Active research program broadly within the disciplines of Earth Surface Materials, Geomorphology, and/or Hydrogeology. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Proven teaching experience at the college/university level.  History of extramural research support.   Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-credit hour teaching load per semester. They are also expected to engage in scholarly activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three credit hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Department: Faculty in the Department of Geology are scholar-teachers and leaders in disciplines as diverse as 1) geochemistry and hydrogeology; 2) geoscience education; 3) sedimentology, paleoclimate and climate science; and 4) geophysics, geodynamics and tectonic processes. We place an emphasis on teaching excellence using inclusive pedagogies, active scholarship with diverse learners, and equitable service to the university and community. The Department of Geology includes nine tenure-track faculty members, two affiliate faculty members, two visiting faculty members and approximately 65 students in our majors (Geology, Geology-Chemistry, Climate Science). We also serve several hundred Integrated Science and Pedagogical Content Knowledge for Elementary Teaching majors with a focus on science education, as well as a new and rapidly growing Environmental Science program. The department values high impact teaching, undergraduate research experiences, and collegial faculty-student interactions (see www.gvsu.edu/geology).   College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest and most intellectually diverse college at Grand Valley State University, offering over 50 bachelor’s degree programs, badges, and certificates spanning the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered learning community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following items to the online application: A single document including: a cover letter addressing qualifications and your motivation to teach at GVSU, curriculum vitae, and the contact information of three references Teaching philosophy statement Research statement (This is a summary of research activities, including those involving undergraduate students, and possible facilities/equipment needs.) Graduate transcripts (Unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable.) Sample of completed or in-progress research activity The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Questions about the position may be directed to search committee chair Associate Professor Ian Winkelstern (winkelsi@gvsu.edu). Application Deadline: Consideration of applicants will begin on November 9, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Violin-and-Viola---Music--Theatre--and-Dance_R105056": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Violin and Viola - Music, Theatre, and Dance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Music, Theatre, and Dance at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Violin and Viola position to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a graduate degree in violin and/or viola performance. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Salary/Benefits: $69,397; commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education: High level of artistry on violin and viola. Graduate degree in violin and/or viola performance. Demonstrated potential to recruit violin and viola students. Potential for successful college-level teaching and ongoing creative activity. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Completed DMA in violin and/or viola performance. Demonstrated success in recruiting and teaching a collegiate violin and/or viola studio. Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-credit hour teaching load per semester. They are also expected to engage in scholarly/creative activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three credit hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly/creative activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Primary duties will include the teaching and mentoring of university level violin and viola students with additional teaching in the core music curriculum as assigned. The teaching assignment will be dependent upon the successful candidate’s qualifications and interests, and the needs of the department.   Department: The Department of Music, Theatre, and Dance provides an intensive performing arts education within a liberal education. Our six-degree programs are offered by 34 full-time faculty with over 150 performances by faculty, guest artists, and students each year. The department is located on the Valley Campus in the Haas Center for the Performing Arts. College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest and most intellectually diverse college at Grand Valley State University, offering over 50 bachelor’s degree programs, badges, and certificates spanning the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered learning community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following items to the online application: A cover letter addressing required and preferred qualifications and your motivation to teach at GVSU. Curriculum vitae which includes contact information for three professional references. Teaching philosophy that includes examples of the candidate’s past successful student recruitment activities. Graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable). A one-page document with YouTube links to a 20-minute performance video and a 20-minute private violin and/or viola lesson or masterclass, both recorded within the past three years. The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Questions about the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Joel Schut (schutjo@gvsu.edu). Application Deadline: Consideration of applicants will begin on October 15, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Microbiology_R105054": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Microbiology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Biomedical Sciences at Grand Valley State University (GVSU) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Microbiology to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in Microbiology or a closely related field. A successful applicant will be expected to: teach introductory microbiology courses and associated labs; develop an active research program at GVSU which engages undergraduate and graduate students; and be a contributing member of the broader community both inside and beyond the institution. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Salary/Benefits: $80,300; commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package , including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. in Microbiology or closely related field. Current ABD candidates will be considered for a two-year contract with provision that the Ph.D. must be completed by December 2028. Evidence of training that has prepared the candidate to teach college microbiology classes. The ability to develop an active research program at GVSU that engages students. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Experience teaching advanced microbiology classes. Post-doctoral research experience.   Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-contact hour teaching load per semester (usually three classes). They are also expected to engage in scholarly activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three contact hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Department: The Department of Biomedical Sciences at Grand Valley State University places an emphasis on teaching excellence using inclusive pedagogies, active scholarship with diverse learners, and equitable service to the university and community. Its 36 full-time faculty uphold the values of a liberal education while providing intensive engagement in the biomedical sciences. High-impact lab experiences in classes and research labs are a hallmark of our relevant, interdisciplinary curriculum. There are numerous collaborative opportunities for faculty and students across a wide group of partners within the department, across campus, and in the community. For more information about the research in the department and the resources available, visit http://www.gvsu.edu/bms . College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest college and intellectually diverse at Grand Valley, offering over 50 bachelor’s degrees and a growing number of advanced degrees in the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following documents to the online application: A cover letter addressing your qualifications and motivation to teach at GVSU. A curriculum vitae with the contact information of three references. A statement of teaching philosophy. A research plan that includes how you will involve students in your research. Graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable). The online application portal will allow you to attach these five documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to an on-campus interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Questions about the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Dr. Doug Graham (grahamdo@gvsu.edu). Application Review: Consideration of applicants will begin on October 19, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    }
